- op: discuss  # bfec
  at: '2026-07-30T15:54:16Z'  # bfec
  by: agent  # bfec
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bfec
  clock: 3  # bfec
  nonce: bfec  # bfec
  message: "SATISFIED — PR #80, dev 26c4258115.\n\nBEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE (the statement is about an exit code, so a proxy would not do): the failure mode was reproduced against the REAL shipped function before the fix and re-run after. The harness sed-extracts summarize_pytest_output + run_pytest out of scripts/smart-test and drives them with a stub pytest that emits \"ERROR <path>\" lines plus \"11756 passed, 2 errors\" and exits 2.\n  BEFORE: run_pytest returned 0 and printed a GREEN CHECK on \"=========== 11756 passed, 2 errors ===========\".\n  AFTER:  run_pytest returns 2 and prints no check.\n  POSITIVE CONTROL: a genuinely green run returns 0 and still prints the check, both before and after — so the harness is proven able to produce either outcome (without this, a harness that always failed would make the guard pass while proving nothing).\n\nROOT CAUSE was the capture, not the greps. The invocation ended with \"> PYTEST_LOG 2>&1 || true\"; after \"cmd || true\" PIPESTATUS reports the \"true\", so exit_code was ALWAYS 0 and pytest's real status was discarded. The log re-derivation then could not distinguish a collection-error run: pytest prints ERROR / \"N errors\" and never \"FAILED \", so neither failure clause fires while the affirmative-green clause (N passed AND NOT N failed) matches. run_pytest returned 0, so the caller's \"TEST_EXIT -eq 0\" gate at smart-test:968 ran the scoped coverage check over a run whose erroring modules never imported, and the final \"exit TEST_EXIT\" carried the false green all the way out. One root cause, one chokepoint.\n\nFIX: preserve pytest's own status, and DELETE the affirmative-green clause rather than repair it — it was a no-op while exit_code started at 0 and would actively mask a real failure now that it does not. It is exactly the \"latent landmine\" the WI-kalub comment beside it named. The two corrective clauses remain but may now only ESCALATE to failure.\n\nDOWNSTREAM CATCH: WI-kalub's Step-1b guard pinned the word-bounded \"[0-9]+ failed\" match INSIDE the clause this fix deletes, so it went red. Rewritten, not resurrected — the 1b property is now satisfied STRUCTURALLY (no failed-matching green clause exists to mis-word-bound), and the test pins the stronger successor property: the exit code is sourced from pytest, and PIPESTATUS may not reappear in code. This is the pt.41(c) shape — fixing a root cause strands its downstream workaround, and the failing test is how you find it.\n\nSCOPE, no weasel words: this closes the false-GREEN direction only. Explicitly NOT addressed and still open: INV-lizor (smart-test skips changes confined to .agent/hooks/ or scripts/, so the per-PR gate is structurally blind to them) — which is why this fix's own tests were run explicitly rather than trusted to slice selection."  # bfec
